---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoToMp3Batch from '@/components/tools/media/VideoToMp3Batch.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="avi-to-mp3">
  <VideoToMp3Batch
    client:load
    from="avi"
    hint="Old camcorder captures and DivX files. The soundtrack comes out as a plain MP3"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Getting sound out of a twenty-year-old file</h2>
    <p>
      AVI files hold audio in many formats, from a time when Windows codecs were installed one by one
      from a CD. FFmpeg has decoders for nearly all of them: MP3, AC-3, PCM, IMA ADPCM, MS ADPCM, GSM,
      μ-law. If your media player cannot open a file, this page can usually still extract its
      soundtrack.
    </p>
    <p>
      The audio is decoded to raw samples and encoded again as MP3 with LAME. If the source was already
      MP3 (common in DivX-era AVI files), this adds a second generation of loss. At 320 kbps, the
      difference is not audible on material of that age.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical reasons people do this</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Rescuing a recording: a camcorder tape capture where the video is unwatchable but the conversation is worth keeping.</li>
      <li>Lecture and seminar archives: shelves of institutional AVI files that people still need to hear but not watch.</li>
      <li>Transcription: speech-to-text tools take audio, not 1.5 GB of video, and mono at 96 kbps is enough.</li>
      <li>Music from an old video collection: concert footage and TV captures whose soundtrack you want on a phone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When it comes out silent</h2>
    <p>
      A few AVI files use audio compressed with a proprietary ACM codec that was a Windows system
      component and was never standardised. No modern decoder includes those codecs, so the audio stream
      cannot be read. The page then reports the problem and does not give you an empty MP3 that looks
      valid. You would find an empty result only later, which is worse than an error.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other cause of silence: some AVI files have no audio stream. The error message tells the two
      cases apart.
    </p>

    <h2>Mono, and when to use it</h2>
    <p>
      Old camcorder audio is often mono, copied to two channels. In that case, "downmix to mono" halves
      the file size with no loss. For real stereo content, such as music or a proper recording setup,
      leave it off.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Source material</th>
            <th>Suggested setting</th>
            <th>One hour becomes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Camcorder voice, single mic</td>
            <td>96 kbps mono</td>
            <td>~41 MB</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Lecture or seminar</td>
            <td>128 kbps mono</td>
            <td>~55 MB</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>TV capture with music</td>
            <td>192 kbps stereo</td>
            <td>~82 MB</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Concert footage</td>
            <td>320 kbps stereo</td>
            <td>~137 MB</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Batch the whole folder</h2>
    <p>
      Drop in all your AVI files at once. They convert one after another, each with a progress row, and
      you can download them as one ZIP at the end. There is no file counter, daily quota or account,
      because no server does the work.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
